You are an expert in interpreting insurance clauses. You will receive a piece of insurance clause content, and then you need to answer questions about that insurance clause content.
Insurance Clause Content:
Article 6 The insurer shall not be liable for the payment of insurance benefits if any of the following circumstances occurs:
(1) Intentional acts of the policyholder or the insured;
(2) Adverse consequences caused by the insured or their family members (see definitions) failing to cooperate with surgery or follow medical advice, and arbitrarily using medication;
(3) The insured or their family members failing to comply with hospital regulations or cooperate with treatment;
(4) When the insured receives a vaccination, they already know or should know that the vaccine used is substandard, or has expired and deteriorated, or has not been approved by the national medical products administration;
(5) Before vaccination, the insured or their guardian failed to truthfully disclose their health status and vaccination contraindications;
(6) The insured failed to receive vaccination on time according to the prescribed procedures;
(7) The vaccination unit violates laws, regulations and normative documents such as the vaccine circulation and immunization management regulations, immunization work standards, and vaccine use guidelines.
Question: If the insured knowingly receives an expired vaccine, will the insurer be liable for the payment of insurance benefits?
Answer:,"If the insured knew that the vaccine had expired when being vaccinated, will the insurer be liable for the payment of insurance benefits?",,,,,,"No liability. According to Article 6, Item 4 of the terms, if the insured knew or should have known that the vaccine was expired or deteriorated at the time of vaccination, the insurer is not liable for paying insurance benefits."

You are an expert in interpreting insurance clauses. You will receive an insurance clause content, and then you need to answer questions about that insurance clause content.
Insurance Clause Content:
Article 8 The insurer shall not be liable for paying insurance benefits for the death, disability, or medical expenses incurred by the insured due to any of the following reasons:
(1) The intentional acts of the policyholder or the insured;
(2) Self-inflicted injury or suicide by the insured, with the exception of the insured being a person without capacity for civil conduct at the time of suicide;
(3) Fighting, being assaulted, or being murdered resulting from the insured's provocation or intentional acts;
(4) Pregnancy, miscarriage, childbirth, illness, drug allergy, heatstroke, or sudden death of the insured;
(5) The insured undergoing cosmetic surgery and other internal or external surgical procedures;
(6) The insured self-administering, applying, or injecting drugs without following medical advice;
(7) Nuclear explosion, nuclear radiation, or nuclear contamination;
(8) The insured committing a crime or resisting arrest;
(9) The insured engaging in high-risk sports or participating in professional or semi-professional sports activities;
(10) Pathological fracture of the insured or diagnosis of osteoporosis and injury resulting from such condition;
(11) The insured having existing fractures prior to applying for insurance;
(12) The insured not suffering a fracture injury, but undergoing bone density testing for other reasons or voluntarily.
Question: If the insured suffers an accidental injury while engaging in high-risk sports, is it within the scope of the exclusions of this insurance contract?
Answer:,"If the insured suffers accidental injury while participating in high-risk sports, does it fall within the scope of exclusions under this insurance contract?",,,,,,"Yes, injuries sustained by the insured while engaging in high-risk sports or participating in professional or semi-professional sports fall within the scope of exclusions of this insurance contract, and the insurer will not bear the liability for paying insurance benefits."

You are an expert in interpreting insurance clauses, and you will receive insurance clause content, then you need to answer questions about the content of the insurance clause.
Insurance Clause Content:
2.4 Exclusions
The Company shall not be liable for the payment of insurance benefits if medical expenses are incurred due to the Insured seeking medical treatment for any of the following circumstances:
(1) Related expenses arising from the Insured's pre-existing conditions (see 7.18) and specially agreed excluded diseases in the policy;
(2) Medical expenses caused by hereditary diseases (see 7.19), congenital deformities, malformations, or chromosomal abnormalities (see 7.20), unless otherwise stipulated in this contract;
(3) Convalescence, vision correction surgery, various health check-up items and preventive medical items, dental care and dental treatment, rehabilitation treatment, cosmetic surgery;
(4) Treatments for the following items: skin pigmentation, acne treatment, rosacea treatment; treatment and removal of freckles, age spots, moles; treatment or surgery for superficial varicose veins, spider veins, scars (other than keloid type), tattoo removal, skin discoloration; laser cosmetic procedures, wrinkle removal, eye bag removal, double eyelid surgery, treatment for alopecia areata, white hair, baldness, hair loss, hair transplantation, hair removal, rhinoplasty, breast augmentation.
Question: Is laser cosmetic treatment for the insured within the scope of insurance liability?
Answer:,Is laser cosmetic treatment undergone by the insured within the scope of insurance liability?,,,,,,"No. According to the terms, laser cosmetic procedures are excluded from coverage, and the company is not liable for paying insurance benefits."

You are an expert in interpreting insurance clauses. You will receive a section of insurance policy content, and then you need to answer questions about that content.
Insurance Policy Content:
Hospital: Refers to public hospitals legally operating within the territory of the People's Republic of China (excluding Hong Kong, Macao, and Taiwan regions) at or above Level II (including Level II), Shanghai Proton and Heavy Ion Center, but does not include sanatoriums, nursing homes, rehabilitation centers, alcohol or drug addiction centers, psychiatric treatment centers, as well as joint hospitals or joint wards of Level II or Level III hospitals without corresponding medical staff or equipment.

Hospitalization: Refers to the Insured being formally admitted to a hospital for treatment due to a critical illness, as diagnosed by a doctor, and requiring continuous treatment in the hospital for more than twenty-four hours. However, it does not include the following situations:
① The Insured's admission to a hospital's outpatient/emergency observation room or home bed (room);
② The Insured's admission to a rehabilitation department, rehabilitation bed, or receipt of rehabilitation treatment;
③ The Insured not receiving examinations and treatments related to the admission diagnosis within one day during hospitalization, or being hospitalized for less than twenty-four hours within one day; except for temporary treatment received at an external hospital as instructed by a doctor;
④ The Insured's hospitalization for a physical examination;
⑤ Bed-occupying hospitalization and other unreasonable hospitalizations.
Question: Does the Insured's admission to a hospital's rehabilitation department for systematic rehabilitation treatment comply with the definition of 'Hospitalization' in this rider?
Answer:,Does the insured's admission to the hospital's rehabilitation department for systematic rehabilitation treatment meet the definition of 'hospitalization' under this supplementary insurance?,,,,,,"Does not meet the criteria. According to the definition of 'hospitalization' in the terms, admission to a rehabilitation department, rehabilitation bed, or receiving rehabilitation treatment is explicitly not covered by this supplementary insurance."

You are an expert in interpreting insurance clauses. You will receive an insurance clause content and then need to answer questions about it.
Insurance Clause Content:
Article 8 The insurer shall not be liable for paying insurance benefits if the insured suffers accidental injury leading to death or disability during the following periods:
(1) During war, military action, riot, or armed rebellion;
(2) While the insured is engaged in illegal or criminal activities, or during lawful detention or imprisonment;
(3) While the insured is intoxicated or under the influence of drugs (see Definitions) or controlled substances;
(4) During periods of mental and behavioral disorders (as defined by the World Health Organization's 'International Statistical Classification of Diseases and Related Health Problems (ICD-10)');
(5) If the insured only purchased ordinary sports accident coverage, but the accident occurred during non-sports activities or high-risk sports activities;
(6) If the insured only purchased high-risk sports accident coverage, but the accident occurred during non-sports activities or ordinary sports activities;
(7) While the insured is driving under the influence of alcohol (see Definitions), driving without a valid driving license (see Definitions), or driving a motor vehicle without a valid driving permit (see Definitions);
Question: If the insured only purchased high-risk sports accident coverage, but an accidental injury occurred during ordinary sports activities, will the insurer bear insurance liability?
Answer:,"If the insured only purchased coverage for high-risk sports accidents, but suffers an accidental injury during ordinary sports activities, will the insurer bear insurance liability?",,,,,,"No liability. According to Article 8, Item 6, if the insured only purchased high-risk sports accident insurance, the insurer is not liable for paying insurance benefits for accidental injuries occurring during ordinary sports activities."
